From: Dan <dan@awto.au>
Subject: [PATCH] net: rteth: two-ring split experiment: PIE CPU-queue rule, zc on both rings, queue 0 threshold, tag QID counters (#146)

- Everything off by default (new module parameters default 0, debugfs only); zc_ring stays 1.
- debugfs `rteth/rxq_pie` (RTL930x): `<sport|dport> <mask> <value> <ring> [udp|tcp|any]` installs one IPv4 PIE rule in an IACL block
  (blocks 8-15) whose only action is the CPU queue id (ACT_MSK_CPUQID r[13] bit 7, QID_DATA r[17] bits 20-24); no forward action, the route trap stays.
  `clear` removes the rules, read lists them. Needs the DSA driver built in; pie.c exports otto_pie_930x_cpuqid_*.
- pie.c: `cpuqid_sel`/`cpuqid_data` in struct pie_rule; the drop-overwrite bit (r[14] bit 14) is skipped for a CPU-QID-only rule.
- zc: `zc_ring` 2 = any RX ring; TX ring = `raw_smp_processor_id() & 1` (zc_ring 0/1: TX ring 0 as before). Doorbell writes only that ring's fetch bit (300-12).
  Counters per RX ring (`ringN_zc_fwd/_miss/_busy/_flowtable_fwd` in rxq, totals keep their names); zc TX pages are reclaimed by the owning ring's NAPI with `tx_by_cpu=1`.
- `cpuq0_drop_pages` (default 0 = keep): queue 0 threshold like 300-40's `cpuq_drop_pages`.
- `rx_qid_stats=1`: frames per ring and CPU tag queue id, rxq lines `tagqid<Q>_ring<R>` (nonzero only); rxq also shows `napi_id_ringN` (thread `napi/eth0-<id>` = ring N).
